#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""unraid-health: daily, strictly READ-ONLY health check of the owner's Unraid server.

How it works
  One SSH call per run: the dispatcher's read-only `health` verb (`unraid-ssh run health`) runs the
  embedded COLLECTOR (bash) server-side.
  It only reads: emhttp state files, /proc, /boot/config, df, sensors, `smartctl -n standby` on
  disks Unraid reports as spinning, docker ps/inspect, virsh list, btrfs device stats, the
  notification and syslog files. Everything is parsed and judged here, in the container.
  Nothing is written on the server; spun-down disks are never touched (Unraid's own cached
  SMART output is used for them). `unraid-health --show-collector` prints exactly what runs there.

Modes (each one collects, compares with the last run, saves state and today's report)
  unraid-health            check: print only NEW issues. Exit 0 = nothing new, 1 = new issues,
                           2 = could not collect (SSH/collector failure).
  unraid-health --report   print the full human-readable report (Markdown)
  unraid-health --json     print the full result as JSON
New issues are texted to the owner (~600 chars) and noted in ~/inbox for the assistant: plain `tg` (out at once,
also at night) when one is critical, a parity check found errors or failed, or the server is unreachable;
else `tg --soft` (waits out quiet hours and a sleep-in morning).
  --no-send   don't text and don't write the inbox note; print them instead (state is still saved,
              so whatever this run reports as new won't be sent later)
  --dry-run   like --no-send, and leave state and reports untouched (pure look)
The first run records a baseline: pre-existing, stable conditions never alert; changes do.
Fullness alerts on entering a higher band (array disks 90/95/98%, pools 90/95%, docker.img 75/90/95%) or when
the array's total free space drops below 3 TB / 1 TB. docker.img growth: fs:docker.img:growth (WARN, once per
episode) when it grew >= 5 GB per 24 h against unraid-capacity's daily sample from 20-48 h before the run.
Degraded array: array:degraded while a parity/data member is disabled, invalid or emulated and no sync rebuilds it:
WARN, CRIT from 7 days on or when an away period (trip-conditions' trips.json; calendar trips of 2+ away nights via
core.away) starts within 14 days or runs. Its start is kept per slot in state (first run: dated from the daily
reports). A slot whose device is gone (DISK_NP_DSBL with a superblock size, an emulated filesystem or an id seen
before) is carried by array:degraded alone; only a slot that never held a disk (no parity2 assigned) is expected.
Counters (CRC, reallocated, btrfs errors...)
alert when they increase; pending/uncorrectable sectors when non-zero. Parity is overdue after the
configured schedule's interval + 7 days (40 days if no schedule), or alerts when a check found errors.
SSH failures alert only after 2 consecutive failed runs. Unraid's own warnings are forwarded once:
not when a later "returned to normal" exists or the disk now reads below its warn temp, and not
again on later runs unless a "returned to normal" came in between (tests: ~/opt/unraid-health-tests). Times are shown in the owner time zone.
Running parity sync / data rebuild (var.ini mdResync*): DISK_INVALID/DSBL on the disk it rebuilds (from
mdResyncAction "recon P|Q|Dn") is INFO with progress, and its "started" notification isn't forwarded. Any
other invalid/disabled disk that still has its device stays CRIT (plus array:degraded); so does the target if it
is still invalid after the sync ends, and a sync that ends with errors is a CRIT event.
Temperatures: array/parity HDDs are hddheat's job (58 °C rule, every 10 min): while hddheat is active (its
runs.log written in the last 2 h) they aren't judged here and Unraid's HDD heat warnings aren't forwarded;
otherwise HDDs warn/crit at hddheat's 58/60 °C (or Unraid's own limits if higher). SSD/NVMe pools warn/crit
at 65/75 °C (or higher Unraid limits): 60-62 °C under sync/mover load is normal. A per-disk limit set in
Unraid always wins. NVMe "Warning Comp. Temperature Time" (minutes above the drive's own warning
threshold) alerts when it rises.
CPU heat: the report always has a line for the last 24 h of cputemp-log (`cputemp-log summary START..END
--json`): max/p95 °C, package-throttled minutes, mean W, P-core MHz. WARN when package throttling exceeds
30 min in 24 h or p95 >= 95 °C (p95 judged with >= 6 h logged); an active WARN clears below 20 min / 92 °C.
No cputemp-log on this box = no line; installed but no rows in 24 h = a data gap.

Acknowledged issues (~/.config/unraid-health/ack.json): a match, an expiry date and a reason. Matching
findings are not new and not in the note/text, but are listed in the report; when the ack expires and the
issue is still there it alerts as new. Acks cover WARN only unless added with --max-level crit.
  unraid-health ack add MATCH --until YYYY-MM-DD --reason TEXT [--max-level warn|crit]
      MATCH: container:NAME (all ctr:NAME:* findings), stopped:NAME (only "exited"/"dead"/"gone" for a
             container that is stopped on purpose; a crash loop or restarts still alert),
             disk:SLOT (findings about that disk/pool), key:GLOB (finding key, fnmatch),
             text:SUBSTR (title, case-insensitive)
      The ack applies while today (owner time zone) is before the --until date (--until never: no expiry).
  A stopped container's last health status ("unhealthy") is stale and is not judged.
  The same ack file also serves unifi-health (key:unifi:* / text:...).
  unraid-health ack list            unraid-health ack rm ID

Files: ~/.local/state/unraid-health/  state.json, last-collect.txt, runs.log, cron.log
       ~/reports/unraid/YYYY-MM-DD.md (one per day, newest 60 kept); ~/.config/unraid-health/ack.json
Testing: --input FILE (saved collector output instead of SSH), --state-dir, --reports-dir,
         --inbox-dir; env UNRAID_HEALTH_HOST (ssh alias), UNRAID_HEALTH_TG (sender, default `tg`),
         UNRAID_HEALTH_NO_SEND=1 (same as --no-send), UNRAID_HEALTH_ACK_FILE, UNRAID_HEALTH_HDDHEAT_LOG,
         UNRAID_HEALTH_CPUTEMP (cputemp-log binary; a missing path = no CPU heat line),
         UNRAID_HEALTH_CAPACITY_SAMPLES (unraid-capacity's samples.jsonl), UNRAID_HEALTH_TRIPS (trips.json),
         UNRAID_HEALTH_CALCACHE (calcache events.json); a missing path = none.
"""

